 boundaries of the superhero genre  and we do that as well in the second  film so I think I think people who love  the first film are gonna be quite  pleased with the second  who are we talking about in terms of  costs and who's returning characters for  kick-ass - well no one is confirmed yet  but I will say that the script is very  much an ensemble piece that focuses on  Dave the kick-ass character and and  Chris red mist who becomes a super  villain in this movie and changes his  name from red mist to the [ __ ]  am I allowed to say that at this  interview and and Mindy who's Hit Girl  and just as she did in the kick-ass 2  book she gives up being Hit Girl the  difference is in the movie we're gonna  spend a lot of time with her and see  what it means when you when you take a  real superhero who and that's what Hit  Girl is and she tries to be a regular  kid sort of the opposite of what Dave  did in the first film you talk about  obviously adapting the comic books here  Howard kind of involved in that side of  the process are you and how do you  envisage it how do you visit it going  well I wrote script so you know I worked  closely with Matthew and with Mark and  would constantly be emailing them and  asking questions but for me it was  really about finding an intersection  between the sequel to the first film and  an adaptation of the comics because you  know if you know the comics well the the  first film takes some liberties right so  if the you know here's the first comic  and here's the first film and then the  second series of the of the books were a  sequel to the the first series I just  sort of find this intersection right  where we were being where we're creating  an honest sequel to the first film but  we're also adapting the books so it's  challenging but I loved it and I loved  the characters and so I had a really  good time doing and actually it came  came to me quite quickly I think I wrote  it in about six weeks did a couple weeks  of work with M